      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Nonopioid and adjuvant analgesics encompass a huge range of heterogenous drugs that differ chemically and mechanistically. These drugs generally are prescribed for mild-to-moderate pain, as coanalgesics for severe pain, or to target specific pain-generating mechanisms. This article provides an overview of some of the more commonly used nonopioid and adjuvant analgesics used to treat chronic pain, including salicylates, acetaminophen, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, tricyclic antidepressants, anticonvulsants, N-Methyl-D-Aspartate receptor antagonists, lidocaine, skeletal muscle relaxants, and topical analgesics."},
